  "prompt_text": "The figure is a Jablonski-style energy diagram summarizing how a molecule can be excited and emit light through several competing pathways. It shows singlet states on the left and triplet states on the right, each with vibrational sublevels. Upward arrows indicate different excitation routes: one-photon absorption from the ground state, two-photon absorption, and higher-state excitation processes labeled ESA and N-PA. After excitation, the molecule relaxes nonradiatively within a manifold and can return to the ground state by fluorescence from the first excited singlet state.\n\nA key feature is the conversion between singlet and triplet manifolds. The diagram shows intersystem crossing from the singlet excited state to the lowest triplet state, followed by phosphorescence back to the ground state. It also highlights reverse intersystem crossing from a higher triplet level back to a singlet state, consistent with delayed-emission or thermally activated pathways.\n\nAdditional arrows marked TA indicate excited-state absorption from triplet levels to higher triplet states. A green downward arrow labeled STED shows stimulated emission depletion driving population from the excited singlet state back to the ground state. Overall, the image compares radiative and nonradiative channels that control molecular photophysics.",
